  "text": "]={\nLet's break down the problem systematically. We start by using the given information about the polynomial roots and their magnitudes.\n\n1. **Degree of Polynomial & Coefficients:**\n   We know that \\(Q(z) = z^3 + sz + t\\) is a cubic polynomial. The coefficients of this polynomial can be derived from Vieta's formulas. Note that the polynomial does not have a constant term (coefficient of \\(z^0\\)), so no constant term doubles the magnitude squared. \n   \n   Hence, the magnitudes squared of the roots satisfy : \n   \\[\n   |x|^2 + |y|^2 + |z|^2 = (-s) \n   \\]\n   Given that \\(|x|^2 + |y|^2 + |z|^2 = 352\\), we can conclude:\n   \\[\n   -s = 352 \\implies s = -352\n   \\]\n\n2. **Isosceles Triangle:**\n   The points corresponding to \\(x, y, z\\) are vertices of an isosceles triangle. Without loss of generality, let \\(|x| = |y| = b.\\) We need to evaluate the distance \\(|z|\\) and use the property of isosceles triangles.\n\n3. **Magnitude Sum and Relation:**\n   Considering polygon properties and armed with information:\n   From \\(|x|^2 + |y|^2 + |z|^2 = 352,\\) and symmetries:\n   \\[\n   2b^2 + |z|^2 = 352 \\implies |z|^2 = 352 - 2b^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Using Magnitudes:**\n   Now we use the context of isosceles trapezoid property derived from angles. Focus on specifics:\n   \\[\n   R(\\theta) x, R(\\theta - 30^\\circ) z, |R(\\theta - 30^\\circ) z|\n   \\]\n   Being essentially vertices, \\( z\\) properties contribute:\n   \\[\n   |z|^2 = 352 - 2b^2\n   \\]\n\nCombining these gives:\n\\[ \n|z|^2 = 352 - 2b^2 \\implies b^2 (1 + 5) = 352 \\implies 6b^2 = 352 \\implies b^2 = \\frac{352}{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\boxed{55.333333333333335}\nPlease review calculations/simplifications! Don't hesitate to ask if revisions needed.amos",
